            margin-top: 8px;
        }

        .read-btn:hover {
            background-color: #164e63;
        }


        /* ===== EXTRA INFORMATION ===== */

        .more-info {
            display: none;
            padding: 0 25px 25px 25px;
            border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
            background-color: #f8fbff;
        }

        .more-info p {
            line-height: 1.8;
            color: #444;
        }

        .more-info h4 {
            color: #164e63;
        }


        /* ===== SUMMARY ===== */

        .summary {
            max-width: 900px;
            margin: 50px auto;
            padding: 30px;
            background-color: #dbeafe;
            border-radius: 15px;
        }

        .summary h2 {
            color: #164e63;
            text-align: center;
        }

        .summary p {
            line-height: 1.8;
        }


        /* ===== FOOTER ===== */

        footer {
            background-color: #123047;
            color: white;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 25px;
            margin-top: 40px;
        }


        /* ===== MOBILE ===== */

        @media (max-width: 700px) {

            header h1 {
                font-size: 32px;
            }

            nav a {
                margin: 0 6px;
                font-size: 13px;
            }

            .card-content {
                flex-direction: column;
                text-align: center;
            }

            .scientist img {
                width: 180px;
                height: 180px;
            }

            .text h3 {
                font-size: 24px;
            }

        }

    </style>
</head>


<body>


    <!-- HEADER -->

    <header id="home">

        <h1>Development of Atomic Theory</h1>

        <p>
            A journey through the ideas and discoveries that helped
            scientists understand the structure and nature of matter.
        </p>

    </header>


    <!-- NAVIGATION -->

    <nav>

        <a href="#home">Home</a>
        <a href="#timeline">Timeline</a>
        <a href="#summary">Summary</a>

    </nav>


    <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="intro">

        <h2>About the Atomic Theory</h2>

        <p>
            The idea of the atom has developed over thousands of years.
            Ancient philosophers tried to explain what matter was made of,
            while later scientists used experiments and observations to
            develop more accurate atomic models.
        </p>

        <p>
            This website presents the important ideas of eleven thinkers,
            beginning with the Ancient Greek philosophers and continuing
            to the development of modern quantum mechanics.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- TIMELINE -->

    <section class="timeline-section" id="timeline">

        <h2>Scientists and Philosophers</h2>


        <!-- 1 THALES -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <!-- PALITAN ANG IMAGE -->
                <img src="thales.jpg"
                     alt="Thales of Miletus">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">01</span>

                    <h3>Thales of Miletus</h3>

                    <div class="year">c. 624–546 BCE</div>

                    <p>
                        Thales of Miletus was an Ancient Greek philosopher
                        who believed that water was the fundamental substance
                        from which everything came.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info1')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info1">

                <h4>Contribution to the idea of matter</h4>

                <p>
                    Thales is considered one of the earliest Greek thinkers
                    to search for a natural explanation of the world instead
                    of explaining everything through mythology. He proposed
                    that water was the basic substance of nature. Although
                    his idea was not an atomic theory, it was important because
                    it encouraged people to ask what everything is made of.
                </p>

                <p>
                    His ideas became part of the early philosophical
                    discussions about the nature of matter and helped
                    influence later thinkers.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 2 EMPEDOCLES -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="empedocles.jpg"
                     alt="Empedocles">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">02</span>

                    <h3>Empedocles</h3>

                    <div class="year">c. 494–434 BCE</div>

                    <p>
                        Empedocles proposed that all matter was composed
                        of four basic elements: earth, water, air, and fire.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info2')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info2">

                <h4>The Four Elements</h4>

                <p>
                    Empedocles believed that the four basic elements were
                    always present and could combine in different ways.
                    Earth represented solid matter, water represented liquids,
                    air represented gases, and fire represented heat and energy.
                </p>

                <p>
                    His theory became influential for many centuries.
                    Although modern science does not accept the four elements
                    as the basic components of matter, his ideas were an
                    important step in the historical development of ideas
                    about matter.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 3 LEUCIPPUS -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="leucippus.jpg"
                     alt="Leucippus">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">03</span>

                    <h3>Leucippus</h3>

                    <div class="year">5th century BCE</div>

                    <p>
                        Leucippus was an Ancient Greek philosopher associated
                        with the early development of atomism.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info3')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info3">

                <h4>Early Atomism</h4>

                <p>
                    Leucippus is traditionally associated with the idea that
                    matter is made of extremely small particles moving through
                    empty space. These particles were thought to be too small
                    to be seen or divided.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This idea was very different from the belief that matter
                    was completely continuous. Leucippus' ideas were later
                    developed further by his student or follower Democritus.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 4 DEMOCRITUS -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="democritus.jpg"
                     alt="Democritus">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">04</span>

                    <h3>Democritus</h3>

                    <div class="year">c. 460–370 BCE</div>

                    <p>
                        Democritus developed the idea that matter was made
                        of tiny particles called "atomos."
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info4')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info4">

                <h4>The Atom</h4>

                <p>
                    Democritus believed that if matter was divided into
                    smaller and smaller pieces, eventually a particle would
                    be reached that could not be divided any further.
                    He called these particles "atomos," which means
                    indivisible.
                </p>

                <p>
                    He suggested that atoms could have different shapes
                    and sizes and that they moved through empty space.
                    His ideas were philosophical rather than experimental,
                    but the concept of atoms became very important in
                    the later development of science.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 5 ARISTOTLE -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="aristotle.jpg"
                     alt="Aristotle">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">05</span>

                    <h3>Aristotle</h3>

                    <div class="year">384–322 BCE</div>

                    <p>
                        Aristotle rejected the idea of indivisible atoms
                        and believed that matter was continuous.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info5')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info5">

                <h4>Aristotle's View of Matter</h4>

                <p>
                    Aristotle supported the idea that matter could be divided
                    continuously and did not consist of tiny indivisible
                    particles. He also supported the concept of four basic
                    elements: earth, water, air, and fire.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Because Aristotle was highly influential, his ideas about
                    matter remained popular for many centuries. This caused
                    the atomic ideas of Democritus to receive less attention
                    for a long period of time.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 6 DALTON -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="images/dalton.jpg"
                     alt="John Dalton">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">06</span>

                    <h3>John Dalton</h3>

                    <div class="year">1766–1844</div>

                    <p>
                        John Dalton developed one of the first modern
                        scientific atomic theories in the early 1800s.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info6')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info6">

                <h4>Dalton's Atomic Theory</h4>

                <p>
                    Dalton proposed that matter is made of atoms and that
                    atoms of each element have their own characteristics.
                    He also proposed that atoms combine in whole-number
                    ratios to form compounds.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Dalton's work was important because his atomic theory
                    was based on scientific observations and chemical
                    evidence. His model represented atoms as solid,
                    indivisible spheres.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 7 THOMSON -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="images/thomson.jpg"
                     alt="J. J. Thomson">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">07</span>

                    <h3>J. J. Thomson</h3>

                    <div class="year">1856–1940</div>

                    <p>
                        J. J. Thomson discovered the electron and showed
                        that atoms contain smaller particles.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info7')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info7">

                <h4>Discovery of the Electron</h4>

                <p>
                    In 1897, J. J. Thomson used experiments with cathode
                    rays to provide evidence for a negatively charged
                    particle that became known as the electron.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This discovery showed that the atom was not indivisible
                    as Dalton's model had suggested. Thomson proposed the
                    "plum pudding" model, where electrons were embedded
                    in a positively charged region.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 8 RUTHERFORD -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="images/rutherford.jpg"
                     alt="Ernest Rutherford">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">08</span>

                    <h3>Ernest Rutherford</h3>

                    <div class="year">1871–1937</div>

                    <p>
                        Rutherford's experiments led to the discovery
                        of the atomic nucleus.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info8')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info8">

                <h4>The Nuclear Model</h4>

                <p>
                    Rutherford and his team performed the famous gold foil
                    experiment. They found that most alpha particles passed
                    through the foil, while a small number were deflected
                    strongly.
                </p>

                <p>
                    From these observations, Rutherford concluded that most
                    of an atom is empty space and that nearly all of its
                    positive charge and most of its mass are concentrated
                    in a very small central nucleus.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 9 BOHR -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="images/bohr.jpg"
                     alt="Niels Bohr">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">09</span>

                    <h3>Niels Bohr</h3>

                    <div class="year">1885–1962</div>

                    <p>
                        Niels Bohr proposed that electrons occupy specific
                        energy levels around the nucleus.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info9')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info9">

                <h4>Bohr's Atomic Model</h4>

                <p>
                    Bohr improved Rutherford's model by proposing that
                    electrons can exist in specific energy levels around
                    the nucleus. Electrons can move from one energy level
                    to another by absorbing or releasing energy.
                </p>

                <p>
                    His model successfully explained important features
                    of the hydrogen atom's spectrum and became an important
                    step toward the development of modern quantum theory.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 10 SCHRODINGER -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="images/schrodinger.jpg"
                     alt="Erwin Schrödinger">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">10</span>

                    <h3>Erwin Schrödinger</h3>

                    <div class="year">1887–1961</div>

                    <p>
                        Schrödinger helped develop the quantum mechanical
                        model of the atom using wave mechanics.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info10')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info10">

                <h4>Quantum Mechanical Model</h4>

                <p>
                    Schrödinger developed a mathematical description of
                    particles using wave functions. His work became an
                    important part of quantum mechanics.
                </p>

                <p>
                    In the modern atomic model, electrons are not described
                    as simply traveling around the nucleus in fixed circular
                    paths. Instead, their locations are described using
                    probabilities. Regions where an electron is likely to
                    be found are called orbitals.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- 11 HEISENBERG -->

        <div class="scientist">

            <div class="card-content">

                <img src="images/heisenberg.jpg"
                     alt="Werner Heisenberg">

                <div class="text">

                    <span class="number">11</span>

                    <h3>Werner Heisenberg</h3>

                    <div class="year">1901–1976</div>

                    <p>
                        Heisenberg made major contributions to quantum
                        mechanics and developed the uncertainty principle.
                    </p>

                    <button class="read-btn"
                            onclick="showInfo('info11')">
                        Read More
                    </button>

                </div>

            </div>


            <div class="more-info" id="info11">

                <h4>Uncertainty Principle</h4>

                <p>
                    Heisenberg developed important ideas in quantum mechanics.
                    His uncertainty principle states that there are limits
                    to how precisely certain pairs of physical properties,
                    such as position and momentum, can be known at the same
                    time.
                </p>

                <p>
                    His work helped establish the modern understanding that
                    electrons cannot be described simply as tiny objects
                    following predictable circular paths around the nucleus.
                    Together with the work of other quantum scientists,
                    Heisenberg helped shape the modern atomic model.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section id="summary">

        <div class="summary">

            <h2>Conclusion</h2>

            <p>
                The development of atomic theory shows how scientific ideas
                change when new evidence and explanations become available.
                Thales began thinking about the basic substance of nature,
                while Empedocles proposed four elements. Leucippus and
                Democritus introduced early ideas about atoms, while
                Aristotle supported a continuous view of matter.
            </p>

            <p>
                Centuries later, John Dalton developed a scientific atomic
                theory. J. J. Thomson discovered the electron, Rutherford
                discovered evidence for the nucleus, and Bohr introduced
                specific energy levels. Schrödinger and Heisenberg then
                contributed to quantum mechanics, which led to the modern
                description of electrons and atoms.
            </p>

            <p>
                Each idea was an important part of the journey toward our
                current understanding of the atom.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- FOOTER -->

    <footer>

        <p>
            <b>Development of Atomic Theory</b>
        </p>

        <p>
